steps.body:
An `async` function always returns a promise. Its return type is `Promise<T>`, where `T` is the type of what it returns, and `await` waits for the promise to settle and gives you that `T`:

```typescript
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

async function double(n: number): Promise<number> {
  await sleep(10);
  return n * 2;
}

const pending = double(21);
//    ^? const pending: Promise<number>
const result = await pending;
//    ^? const result: number
console.log(result); // 42
```

`node:timers/promises` exports a `setTimeout` that returns a promise. Inside the import braces, `as` only renames it to `sleep`, so it isn't a type assertion. `await sleep(10)` pauses for 10 milliseconds.

`main.ts` is an ES module, so `await` also works at the top level, outside any function. Annotate the return type of async functions that other code calls, as you would any other function. It must be a `Promise`: writing `number` there is TS1064, because the function really returns a promise.

examples.code:
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

type User = { id: number; name: string; email: string };

const USERS = new Map<number, User>([
  [1, { id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" }],
  [2, { id: 2, name: "Grace", email: "grace@example.com" }],
]);

async function loadUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  await sleep(10);
  const user = USERS.get(id);
  if (user === undefined) {
    throw new Error(`no user ${id}`);
  }
  return user;
}

async function greeting(id: number): Promise<string> {
  const user = await loadUser(id);
  //    ^? const user: User
  return `Hello, ${user.name} <${user.email}>`;
}

greeting(2).then((text) => {
  //              ^? (parameter) text: string
  console.log(text);
});

