steps.body:
You can't cancel a promise from outside. Instead, async APIs accept an `AbortSignal`, an object that says when to give up. `AbortSignal.timeout(ms)` makes a signal that aborts after `ms` milliseconds. Pass it as the `signal` option, and the API rejects as soon as the signal aborts:

```typescript
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

try {
  await sleep(1000, undefined, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(20) });
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err instanceof Error ? err.name : String(err)); // AbortError
}
```

`sleep` takes its options as the third argument. `fetch`, `readFile` from `node:fs/promises`, and many other Node APIs take a `signal` too.

Your own async functions can take a `signal: AbortSignal` parameter and pass it on to the APIs they call. Between steps of their own work, they can call `signal.throwIfAborted()`, which throws the signal's reason once it has aborted. `signal.aborted` is `true` from then on. A function that takes a signal should stop soon after it aborts, so check the signal inside long loops.

examples.code:
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

async function wait(label: string, ms: number, limitMs: number): Promise<void> {
  try {
    await sleep(ms, undefined, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(limitMs) });
    console.log(`${label}: done after ${ms} ms`);
  } catch (err) {
    const name = err instanceof Error ? err.name : "unknown error";
    console.log(`${label}: ${name} after ${limitMs} ms`);
  }
}

await wait("quick", 10, 200);
await wait("slow", 1000, 50);

import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

type Step = { name: string; ms: number };

const steps: Step[] = [
  { name: "load rows", ms: 10 },
  { name: "add totals", ms: 10 },
  { name: "render PDF", ms: 400 },
  { name: "email it", ms: 10 },
];

async function buildReport(signal: AbortSignal): Promise<number> {
  let done = 0;
  for (const step of steps) {
    signal.throwIfAborted();
    await sleep(step.ms);
    console.log(`finished ${step.name}`);
    done++;
  }
  return done;
}

try {
  const done = await buildReport(AbortSignal.timeout(200));
  console.log(`report ready after ${done} steps`);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(`report stopped: ${err instanceof Error ? err.name : String(err)}`);
}

import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

async function withTimeout<T>(
  work: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>,
  ms: number,
): Promise<T | "timeout"> {
  const signal = AbortSignal.timeout(ms);
  try {
    return await work(signal);
  } catch (err) {
    if (signal.aborted) {
      return "timeout";
    }
    throw err;
  }
}

async function fetchStock(delayMs: number, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<number> {
  await sleep(delayMs, undefined, { signal });
  return 12;
}

const fast = await withTimeout((signal) => fetchStock(10, signal), 100);
//    ^? const fast: number | "timeout"
console.log(`fast: ${fast}`);

const slow = await withTimeout((signal) => fetchStock(1000, signal), 100);
if (slow === "timeout") {
  console.log("slow: gave up after 100 ms");
} else {
  console.log(`slow: ${slow.toFixed(0)} in stock`);
}

