steps.body:
A generator function, `function*`, produces values one at a time with `yield`. An async generator, `async function*`, can also `await` between values, which suits data that arrives over time, like pages from an API. Its return type is `AsyncGenerator<T>`, where `T` is the type of each value it yields. Read one with `for await`, which waits for each value in turn:

```typescript
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

async function* ticks(count: number): AsyncGenerator<number> {
  for (let i = 1; i <= count; i++) {
    await sleep(5);
    yield i;
  }
}

for await (const tick of ticks(2)) {
  console.log(`tick ${tick}`);
}
console.log(await Array.fromAsync(ticks(3))); // [ 1, 2, 3 ]
```

A generator's code runs only when you ask for the next value, and `break` out of a `for await` loop stops it for good. `Array.fromAsync` reads a generator to the end and resolves to an array of every value, as the last line shows.

examples.code:
import { setTimeout as sleep } from "node:timers/promises";

async function* countdown(from: number): AsyncGenerator<number> {
  for (let n = from; n > 0; n--) {
    await sleep(10);
    yield n;
  }
}

for await (const n of countdown(3)) {
  //             ^? const n: number
  console.log(n);
}
console.log("liftoff");

